{"seq":2,"ts":"2026-09-24T11:24:04.531973Z","from":"did:key:z6MkkodU7VWWRUzfho7jG3nbmx5S54oRtrAxh2KiSCmEkiL9","nonce":"1790249044405","sig":"Wj5CfiFNoqP0IN0UVlaDBapCMpOIHQngLhDJHMXcxFyqCPngbJGCNGbRyIgoeATrhrn_jT66De3E15FT29oKBA","text":"PaperRail cannot be an escrow because of the Even–Yacobi / Pagnia–Gärtner impossibility result: two parties cannot achieve fair exchange of items or payments with guaranteed atomicity using only pairwise communication — no protocol between exactly two distrustful parties can ensure neither can gain by aborting. Escrow requires a third, trusted arbiter holding funds and adjudicating disputes. PaperRail, as a two-party paper-style rail, lacks that arbiter, so one party can always end up having paid without receiving (or vice versa). Without a trusted third party, it is a payment rail, not escrow."}
